    {
        "user": "ULSDVUUVA",
        "type": "message",
        "ts": "1567083572.010800",
        "client_msg_id": "4460a384-db59-48cc-b36f-aee30b889b31",
        "text": "<https:\/\/kenko.tech\/measuring-emotions> Nowadays, the dimensional approach (Bradley, 1994; Lang, Dhillon, &amp; Dong, 1995) conceptualizes emotion as having two or sometimes even three basic dimensions along which the entire range of human emotions can be arranged. The most common dimensions are valence (which ranges from happy to sad) and arousal (which ranges from calm to excited) which can be mapped to most of the emotions. The third less often mentioned dimension is dominance (ranging from in control to out of control).",
        "team": "T03FC4NDZ",
        "user_team": "T03FC4NDZ",
        "source_team": "T03FC4NDZ",
        "user_profile": {
            "avatar_hash": "d6568f25a9a4",
            "image_72": "https:\/\/avatars.slack-edge.com\/2022-02-08\/3075305835828_d6568f25a9a41b2be2ef_72.jpg",
            "first_name": "Maike",
            "real_name": "Maike",
            "display_name": "Maike",
            "team": "T03FC4NDZ",
            "name": "maikeherbort",
            "is_restricted": false,
            "is_ultra_restricted": false
        },
        "thread_ts": "1566477869.021400",
        "parent_user_id": "ULSDVUUVA",
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "1od",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "link",
                                "url": "https:\/\/kenko.tech\/measuring-emotions"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": " Nowadays, the dimensional approach (Bradley, 1994; Lang, Dhillon, & Dong, 1995) conceptualizes emotion as having two or sometimes even three basic dimensions along which the entire range of human emotions can be arranged. The most common dimensions are valence (which ranges from happy to sad) and arousal (which ranges from calm to excited) which can be mapped to most of the emotions. The third less often mentioned dimension is dominance (ranging from in control to out of control)."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "user": "ULSDVUUVA",
        "type": "message",
        "ts": "1567083600.011000",
        "client_msg_id": "21b9d22f-5032-4397-a331-9e0d6704aa49",
        "text": "As shown in Fig. 4, SAM ranges from a smiling, happy figure to a frowning, unhappy figure when representing the valence dimension (top panel), and ranges from an excited, wide-eyed figure to a relaxed, sleepy figure for the arousal dimension (middle panel). The dominance dimension represents changes in control with changes in the size of SAM: a large figure indicates maximum control in the situation (bottom panel). In this version of SAM, the subject can place an ‘x’ over any of the five figures in each scale, or between any two figures, which results in a 9-point rating scale for each dimension. In another version, the subject can choose only one of the five figures in each scale, results in a 5-point rating scale for each dimension.",
        "team": "T03FC4NDZ",
        "user_team": "T03FC4NDZ",
        "source_team": "T03FC4NDZ",
        "user_profile": {
            "avatar_hash": "d6568f25a9a4",
            "image_72": "https:\/\/avatars.slack-edge.com\/2022-02-08\/3075305835828_d6568f25a9a41b2be2ef_72.jpg",
            "first_name": "Maike",
            "real_name": "Maike",
            "display_name": "Maike",
            "team": "T03FC4NDZ",
            "name": "maikeherbort",
            "is_restricted": false,
            "is_ultra_restricted": false
        },
        "thread_ts": "1566477869.021400",
        "parent_user_id": "ULSDVUUVA",
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "aogWB",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "As shown in Fig. 4, SAM ranges from a smiling, happy figure to a frowning, unhappy figure when representing the valence dimension (top panel), and ranges from an excited, wide-eyed figure to a relaxed, sleepy figure for the arousal dimension (middle panel). The dominance dimension represents changes in control with changes in the size of SAM: a large figure indicates maximum control in the situation (bottom panel). In this version of SAM, the subject can place an ‘x’ over any of the five figures in each scale, or between any two figures, which results in a 9-point rating scale for each dimension. In another version, the subject can choose only one of the five figures in each scale, results in a 5-point rating scale for each dimension."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "user": "ULSDVUUVA",
        "type": "message",
